{"packet_id":"D065-01","doc_id":"D065","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Fair Chance in Housing Act criminal record inquiry limits","requirement":"A housing provider may not ask about an applicant's criminal record before a conditional offer, may consider only specified convictions afterward, and may not require drug or alcohol tests; withdrawal of an offer requires written notice and an individualized assessment.","key_value":"6-year lookback for first degree, 4-year for second/third, 1-year for fourth","coverage_conditions":"Rental dwelling units offered for rent by a housing provider for residential purposes; does not apply to a dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied premises","unit_limit":4}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["“Rental dwelling unit” means a dwelling unit offered for rent by a housing provider for residential purposes, other than a dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units."]},"exemptions":"Dwelling units in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units. A housing provider may also consider methamphetamine-manufacture convictions in federally assisted housing and lifetime sex-offender registration.","penalty":"Up to $1,000 for a first violation, $5,000 with one prior violation in five years, and $10,000 with two or more priors in seven years; the director may order compliance steps and pay up to $1,000 to the applicant.","effective_date":"2022-01-01","valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-55","quoted_span":"A housing provider shall not require an applicant to complete any housing application that includes any inquiries regarding an applicant’s criminal record prior to the provision of a conditional offer","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D065-01","doc_id":"D065","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Fair Chance in Housing Act application fee disclosure and refund","requirement":"Before accepting any application fee, a housing provider must disclose in writing whether its eligibility criteria include criminal history review and that the applicant may submit evidence of record inaccuracies, rehabilitation or other mitigating factors.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rental dwelling units offered for rent by a housing provider for residential purposes; does not apply to a dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied premises","unit_limit":4}],"per_tenancy":null,"coverage_quotes":["“Rental dwelling unit” means a dwelling unit offered for rent by a housing provider for residential purposes, other than a dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units."]},"exemptions":"Dwelling units in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units.","penalty":"If a violation is substantiated, the director may require the applicant's rental application fee be returned and may order up to $1,000 of the penalty paid to the applicant.","effective_date":"2022-01-01","valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-55","quoted_span":"Prior to accepting any application fee, a housing provider shall disclose in writing to the applicant: (1) Whether the eligibility criteria of the housing provider include the review and consideration of criminal history; and (2) A statement that the applicant, pursuant to subsection a. of this section, may provide evidence demonstrating inaccuracies within the applicant's criminal record or evidence of rehabilitation or other mitigating factors.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D065-01","doc_id":"D065","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Fair Chance in Housing Act eviction after successful provider appeal","requirement":"If a housing provider's appeal succeeds and the director's decision is overturned, the provider may evict the former applicant housed under that order, after giving at least 45 days' notice.","key_value":"45 days' notice","coverage_conditions":"A former applicant residing in a rental dwelling unit owned by the housing provider as the result of the director's order.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied premises","unit_limit":4}],"per_tenancy":"Applies only where the housing provider's appeal succeeds and the court overturns the director's final decision that resulted in the order to house the applicant.","coverage_quotes":["“Rental dwelling unit” means a dwelling unit offered for rent by a housing provider for residential purposes, other than a dwelling unit in an owner-occupied premises of not more than four dwelling units."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":"2022-01-01","valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-63","quoted_span":"then a determination that the housing provider did not violate the provisions of this act as evidenced by such successful appeal shall be grounds for the housing provider to evict the former applicant if that person resides in a rental dwelling unit owned by the housing provider as the result of the director’s order, so long as the housing provider provides the applicant with at least 45 days’ notice prior to the eviction","interaction":"Operates notwithstanding any provision of the Anti-Eviction Act, P.L.1974, c.49 to the contrary","relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
